//
// The index POINTS at a commit. It never mirrors what git already holds.

release "REL.0.8.0" {
    version "0.8.0"
    state "withdrawn"
    withdrawn-because #"""
        Cut to exercise the chain, which had never run end to end (AA4, AF2, WALK.260822.02).
        It ran, and found two defects and a product judgement.

        The judgement is why this is withdrawn rather than kept: the four documents this cut
        froze are correct and not worth reading (AS4). release-notes.md is a hundred and
        seventy lines of iteration ids; the guide says nothing shipped while five capabilities
        are active. Archival immutability would make that the permanent record of what 0.8.0
        was, and 0.8.0 has been tagged for nobody.

        Withdrawn rather than bypassed. A flag to republish over a cut release would be a
        permanent escape hatch bought to solve a development problem, and the record already
        has the right move for a claim that should not stand — S1 was withdrawn, not
        grandfathered. The index below is what WAS cut, kept so that this and a version never
        cut at all do not read alike.

        Re-cut when TS.260821.12 has landed and the published set is worth opening.
        """#
    binds "ITER.260820.01"
